                    briefing_text_r1:       `Duramax's audited statements show <strong>€14.2M EBITDA</strong> on €88M revenue. The sponsor's bridge adds <strong>€3.3M</strong> to reach a “€17.5M adjusted EBITDA” — a <em>€2.1M related-party “management fee”</em> and <em>€1.2M of “one-off” restructuring</em> that has appeared three years running. The auditors signed neither. Using a financial-information-quality lens, decide which add-backs survive, judge the <strong>receivables that crept from 52 to 71 days</strong>, and set a defensible underwriting EBITDA. Every downstream number — leverage, PD, price — rests on this one.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Score the default risk`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Off your underwriting EBITDA, build the credit picture: <strong>leverage</strong> (Debt/EBITDA), interest cover and debt-service cover, then map Duramax to an internal rating and a <strong>PD band</strong>. On the audited €14.2M, leverage is <em>3.2×</em>, not the sponsor's 2.6×. Then <strong>stress it</strong>: tiles demand tracks construction, and a 25–30% EBITDA decline in year 2–3 is a real scenario. Does coverage survive? Name the single factor that most drives this credit's risk.`,
