                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_reject:        `<strong>That's underwriting, not order-taking.</strong> You rejected both add-backs and lent against the audited €14.2M. The €2.1M related-party fee and the recurring “one-off” restructuring were never signed by the auditors — strip them and leverage isn't 2.6×, it's <em>3.2×</em>. You priced the risk you can see, not the risk the seller wants you to see.`,
                    coach_r1_partial:       `<strong>Splitting the difference still lends against unsigned earnings.</strong> €15.9M is a negotiation, not an analysis. A €2.1M related-party “management fee” is either a real cost of the business or it isn't — halving it just books half a fiction. The committee will ask which specific euro of the €3.3M you can defend.`,
                    coach_r1_accept:        `<strong>You underwrote to the sponsor's €17.5M.</strong> But €3.3M of that is add-backs your auditors never signed — a related-party fee and “one-off” costs that recur three years running. On the real €14.2M, leverage is <em>3.2×</em>, not 2.6×, and every downstream number is now wrong. Earnings quality precedes everything.`,
                    coach_r1_receivables:   `<strong>You waved off the receivables drift.</strong> DSO climbing from 52 to 71 days is cash the company has booked as profit but not collected — a classic earnings-quality flag. In a buy-out, that working-capital gap is exactly where a “profitable” borrower runs out of liquidity first.`,
