                    coach_r2_ratingsoft:    `<strong>BB at PD ~1.5% is the sponsor's rating, not yours.</strong> That band belongs to 2.6× leverage on the inflated €17.5M. On the earnings you can defend, this is a B credit at best — rate it soft and you'll price it soft.`,
                    coach_r2_stressfull:    `<strong>You modelled the real downturn.</strong> Tiles demand tracks construction; a −28% EBITDA hit in year 2–3 is not pessimism, it's the cycle. Confirming interest cover survives that scenario is what separates a credit decision from a hope.`,
                    coach_r2_nostress:      `<strong>You skipped the stress test.</strong> A 25–30% cyclical EBITDA decline is the scenario that breaks this loan in year 2–3 — and you've left it unmodelled. Unmodelled cyclicality is uncompensated risk: it doesn't show up in the price, it shows up in the workout.`,
                    coach_r2_driverright:   `<strong>You named the real driver.</strong> Leverage on true earnings times cyclicality is the whole risk here — the gap between 3.2× and 2.6× <em>is</em> the deal. Get that right and price and covenants follow; get it wrong and nothing downstream is safe.`,
                    coach_r2_drivermargin:  `<strong>“The margin is healthy, so risk is low” is the seller's logic.</strong> A 16.1% EBITDA margin tells you the business is profitable today — it tells you nothing about leverage on disputed earnings or what a construction downturn does to coverage. Margin is not the risk driver; leverage × cyclicality is.`,
                    coach_r2_driversponsor: `<strong>A repeat sponsor is a relationship, not a credit enhancement.</strong> The sponsor doesn't repay the loan — Duramax's cash flow does. Leaning on reputation is exactly how desks underwrite the relationship instead of the borrower.`,
                    coach_r3_priceright:    `<strong>You priced toward the model, not the ask.</strong> At this margin the RAROC starts to clear expected loss and the capital charge — which is the only test that matters. Holding above the sponsor's 350 is what makes this a loan that creates value rather than destroys it.`,
                    coach_r3_underprice:    `<strong>You matched the sponsor's ~350 over Euribor.</strong> Run the RAROC: at this PD and LGD, expected loss alone eats most of that margin, and you're below the desk's 12% hurdle before the capital charge. You didn't win a deal — you booked a loan that destroys economic value.`,
                    coach_r3_covlite:       `<strong>Covenant-lite on a cyclical LBO is flying blind.</strong> One generous interest-cover test, and when tiles demand turns down 28% nothing trips until the equity cushion is already gone. By the time you find out, you're an unsecured creditor in a workout. A leverage covenant would have put you at the table six months earlier.`,
                    coach_r3_nostructure:   `<strong>A bullet with no equity condition leaves your loss-given-default fully exposed.</strong> Amortisation de-levers the bullet risk; a real equity cushion is what stands between you and the loss if the downturn hits. Price protects the expected loss — structure protects the unexpected one.`,
                    coach_r3_structured:    `<strong>That's a financed risk, not a hoped-for one.</strong> Priced above the hurdle, a full maintenance covenant package with a cash-sweep, and a 40% equity cushion with amortisation — the RAROC clears and the structure gives you early warning and a real recovery. This is the term sheet a committee approves.`,
                    coach_r4_full:          `<strong>Strong standalone analysis — and you just took the book from 84% to 88% of limit.</strong> Almost all of it cyclical industrials. One construction downturn now hits this loan <em>and</em> a third of your existing exposures at once. Was this deal worth the last of your risk budget, or should you have reduced your hold?`,
                    coach_r4_decline:       `<strong>You passed. Safe — and you handed a profitable repeat sponsor to a rival over problems you could have priced and covenanted around.</strong> Underwriting isn't yes/no; it's “yes, if.” What conditions — higher margin, tighter covenant, equity top-up, reduced hold — would have made this a deal you <em>could</em> do?`,
                    coach_r4_conditions:    `<strong>“Yes, if” — that's underwriting.</strong> You'll fund it only at your price, your covenants and an equity cushion. The sponsor may push back, but a deal done on your terms inside a priced, covenanted structure is a deal the desk can stand behind. Watch the concentration — at 88% of limit you've spent the last of the budget.`,
                    coach_r4_reducedhold:   `<strong>Reduced hold is the portfolio answer.</strong> Keep €25M, syndicate €20M: you bank the relationship and the fees, hold the book closer to the limit, and dilute the cyclical concentration. The marginal deal competes for scarce risk budget — and you just made room without walking away.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchright:    `<strong>You led the committee with the right number.</strong> RAROC after expected loss and capital, the covenant strength, and the portfolio fit — not the headline margin or the relationship. That's the case a credit committee can approve, because it answers the question the bank actually faces.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchmargin:   `<strong>Leading with the headline spread invites the obvious challenge.</strong> The committee will ask what's left of that margin after expected loss and the capital charge — and if you've downplayed the EL, the answer is “not enough.” Spread is revenue; RAROC is the return.`,
                    coach_r4_pitchrel:      `<strong>Leading with the relationship is how desks talk themselves into bad loans.</strong> A fee-rich repeat sponsor is a reason to want the deal, not a reason it's a good credit. The committee underwrites the borrower's cash flow, not the relationship's history.`,
                    coach_r4_appetitewin:   `<strong>“A no costs more than any default” is exactly how the next default gets booked.</strong> Win-the-mandate appetite prices to the client, not the risk. One default in a near-full, concentrated book costs the desk far more than one lost mandate.`,
                    coach_r4_appetiteavoid: `<strong>“When in doubt, decline” avoids loss and creates no value.</strong> A desk that only books the loans with no doubt books almost nothing — and hands every structurable deal to a competitor. The skill is lending the risk you can price, not avoiding all risk.`,

